refs.extend([dict(id='eccc',title='SOS lower bound for exact planted clique — version record',authors='Shuo Pang',year=2021,url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2021/070/',locator='TR21-070, 13 May 2021; abstract and revision record checked 12 September 2026.'),dict(id='author',title='Shuo Pang — research papers',authors='Shuo Pang',year=None,url='https://shuopang.net/',locator='Checked 12 September 2026; the exact-clique paper remains listed alongside later work on other proof-complexity problems.')])
issue='The source asks for the best meaningful degree lower bound as p varies, especially for small p, but does not select the relationship between p(n) and the target clique size k(n), a nontrivial unsatisfiable parameter range, or the precision of an acceptable bound. The random-graph and exact SOS models are recoverable; a particular asymptotic target is not. Choosing a sparse regime or demanding a complete constant-factor phase diagram would add a target not selected in the source.'
save(i,dict(title='Exact-clique SOS degree lower bounds in G(n,p)',formal='For graphs G sampled from G(n,p), what degree lower bounds hold with high probability for sum-of-squares refutations of the existence of a clique of exactly k vertices, when the edge probability p is allowed to differ from 1/2 and to depend on n? The source particularly asks about small p and suitable clique sizes. Its exact problem keeps the equality ∑ᵢxᵢ=k and all its allowed polynomial consequences. The parameter regime and requested sharpness of the lower bound remain unspecified.',definitions='G(n,p) is the distribution of simple undirected graphs on [n] in which each unordered pair is independently an edge with probability p. Here 0<p<1 and 1≤k≤n is an integer target clique size. No clique is planted in the sampled graph; “planted clique” in the paper’s title refers to the algorithmic motivation. For a fixed G, the polynomial equations over R are xᵢ²−xᵢ=0 for every i, xᵢxⱼ=0 for each nonedge {i,j}, and ∑ᵢxᵢ−k=0. A degree-d SOS refutation is a polynomial identity −1=∑_a q_a F_a+∑_b r_b², where F_a run over these equations’ left sides, the multipliers q_a and r_b have real coefficients, deg(q_a F_a)≤d, and 2deg(r_b)≤d. Proof size and coefficient bit length are not the cost being asked about. Equivalently for a sufficient lower-bound certificate, a degree-d pseudoexpectation is a linear functional L on polynomials of degree at most d with L(1)=1, L(q²)≥0 whenever 2deg(q)≤d, and L(qF_a)=0 whenever deg(qF_a)≤d. In particular, the size equality must hold after multiplication by every admissible q; requiring only L(∑ᵢxᵢ)≥k is the weaker non-exact formulation. Write D(G,k) for the minimum refutation degree and set D=∞ when no refutation exists. A high-probability degree lower bound d(n) means Pr[D(G,k(n))≥d(n)]→1 as n→∞, with G∼G(n,p(n)). To express a nontrivial refutation lower bound, the chosen regime should also have no k(n)-clique with probability tending to one; satisfiable instances admit no refutation for the elementary reason that their equations have a solution.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='After specifying a source-compatible regime for p(n), k(n), the desired lower-bound scale and probability guarantee, prove that every exact-clique SOS refutation has at least that degree with the required probability. A result only for the weaker expected-size inequality, only for p=1/2, or relying on the typical existence of an actual k-clique does not answer the intended extension.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Exact cardinality strengthens the relaxation',text='The source distinguishes enforcing the exact number of chosen vertices through all low-degree polynomial consequences from placing just one lower bound on the expected number. Lower bounds for the weaker system need not survive these additional equality constraints.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The established theorem uses edge probability one half',text='For G(n,1/2) and target size n^{1/2−ε}, the source establishes an SOS degree lower bound of order ε² log n/log log n in its stated range, with a capped absolute constant when needed. Question (2) asks how degree bounds should depend on a different, possibly small edge probability.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The extracted fragment referred to a density extension',text='“Exact” identifies the cardinality-equality model within the preceding question about G(n,p). The isolated fragment did not ask whether the paper’s already proved p=1/2 exact-case theorem could be obtained.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Remaining specification',text=issue)],why='Would determine how random-graph density affects the power of semidefinite proof systems when the target cardinality is enforced exactly, rather than through a weaker optimization constraint.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2021',text='Theorem 1.4 gives the exact-cardinality degree lower bound for G(n,1/2); Section 7 asks about varying p, especially small p.',citation='primary')],status='source_open',status_note='Read both the exact proof-system definitions and the complete concluding question. Checked the ECCC record, the author’s current papers, and later searches through 12 September 2026. No resolution of this broad density-dependent extension was identified; the source leaves its quantitative target open.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',remaining_issue=issue,notes=['Recovered the G(n,p) antecedent and the exact cardinality constraints.']),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Tests how equality constraints and graph density affect a widely used algorithmic proof system. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Replaced the four-word fragment by its density-dependent exact-SOS question; defined the graph distribution, refutation degree and high-probability interpretation, and retained the missing parameter target.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The input is an ordinary random graph with independent edge probability p.','The proof system tries to refute a clique of a specified exact size using polynomial equations and sums of squares.','The exact size equation must hold under all permitted polynomial multipliers, unlike the weaker expected-size relaxation.','The source proves strong degree lower bounds at p=1/2 and asks for corresponding bounds at other densities, especially sparse ones.','It does not choose the relationship between density and clique size or the required precision of those bounds.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','eccc','author'])),notes=['Recovered the missing G(n,p) context.','Defined the exact SOS equations, degree measure and distributional quantifiers.','Separated genuine refutation hardness from satisfiable instances and the weaker expected-size model.','Recorded the absent parameter regime and precision without inventing a conjecture.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')

